# tools/ales_stereo_notch_witness.rish — stereo_notch: the band-reject carried into stereo, the same two cutoffs on both channels, each channel a stateless parallel sum — the fourth rung of the stereo EQ / filter class (ALES173).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES173.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-080408_fill-ales173-lotus-stereo-notch.md
# Stands on: lotus/notch.rye (ALES47 — the mono band-reject run once per channel, its ToneError reused whole), lotus/tone.rye (ALES40 — the low-pass and high-pass the notch sums and the precheck sharing one validation),
# lotus/band.rye (ALES46 — the band-pass this notch complements), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate the notch's sum reuses).
#
# lotus/stereo_notch.rye lifts a reject band from a stereo master: it runs ALES47's proven mono band-reject on BOTH channels with the SAME two cutoffs — keep the low band (a low-pass at the low cutoff) and the high band (a
# high-pass at the high cutoff), and write their sum saturated once; what neither branch keeps, the middle band between the cutoffs, is rejected. A notch's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the
# field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each channel notches its own content, and like the shelf and unlike the band-pass there is no carried state to thread. It validates both
# cutoffs and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadCoeff on an illegal cutoff, BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never notches one channel and leaves the other torn;
# ToneError reused whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo notch law (each channel equal to ALES47's mono notch with the same two cutoffs byte for byte, the channels
# genuinely differing), the zero-width / direct-current law (equal cutoffs the identity on both, a settled constant surviving per channel), the complement law (neither low-pass nor high-pass alone, distinct from ALES172's
# band-pass), and the image / balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_notch_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-notch: Language EN — stereo_notch lifts a reject band from a stereo master by running ALES47's proven mono band-reject on both channels with the same two cutoffs: keep the low band (a low-pass at the low cutoff) and the high band (a high-pass at the high cutoff), and write their sum saturated once; what neither branch keeps, the middle band between the cutoffs, is rejected. A notch's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each channel notches its own content, and like the shelf and unlike the band-pass there is no carried state to thread. It validates both cutoffs and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never notches one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-notch: Style Radiant — the stereo EQ and filter class now has its tone control (ALES170, one edge kept), its two-band shelf (ALES171, both bands tilted), and its band-pass (ALES172, the middle isolated); the exact complement of the band-pass is the notch — keep the edges, drop the middle. It is the surgical cut a keeper reaches for: the mains hum pulled from a take, the resonant ring notched from a room, the mud scooped while the lows and the air stay whole. ALES47 built that for a mono clip: sum, in parallel, a low-pass at the low cutoff and a high-pass at the high cutoff, and write the sum saturated once over the true wide-domain total; what neither branch keeps, the middle band, is rejected. This rung carries it into stereo the way the EQ class asks — run ALES47's proven notch on each channel with the same two cutoffs, so the reject band lifts on both speakers together. Like every parametric edit, a notch's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for free, with no linking and no measurement; each channel notches its own content, which is exactly right for equalization — an identical-channel, mono-in-stereo master stays identical because the same input through the same cutoffs is the same deterministic output, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. Like the shelf and unlike the band-pass, there is no carried state to lift: the notch runs a fresh low-pass and high-pass over the whole span each call, so a stereo notch is a stateless per-channel pass with nothing to thread across a seam. The crux is four laws: the stereo notch law (each channel equal to ALES47's mono notch with the same two cutoffs byte for byte, the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed); the zero-width and direct-current law (equal cutoffs reconstructing the input byte for byte on both, the reject band of zero width the identity, and a settled constant surviving on both as the notch keeps direct current); the complement law (the notch genuinely neither the low-pass nor the high-pass alone, and distinct from ALES172's band-pass on the same cutoffs, keeping the edges rather than the middle); and the image, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the notch, an all-silent master notching to silence on both, the lengths held, an illegal low cutoff, an illegal high cutoff, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-notch: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each notched through ALES47's own two scratch bands, every band sum run in i64 so nothing overflows before the single clamp per channel; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a notch writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); both cutoffs and the span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never notches one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoNotchError is ALES47's ToneError (BadCoeff, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo notch law is proven by running both stereo_notch and ALES47's mono notch on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the zero-width and direct-current law proves equal cutoffs are the input byte for byte on both and that a settled constant survives on each channel; the complement law proves the notch is neither the input, nor a plain low-pass, nor a plain high-pass, nor ALES172's band-pass on the same cutoffs; and the image, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an identical-channel master staying identical, an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the three refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the cutoffs over sample indices rather than crossovers in hertz, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_notch"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_notch.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-notch: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_notch.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-notch: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-notch: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-notch' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-notch: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-notch line"
